explanation:
The definition (line 635) is faithful. `Under X` is defined as `StructuredArrow X (𝟭 T)`, which is the comma category `Comma (fromPUnit X) (𝟭 T)`. `Under.Hom f g` is just `CommaMorphism f g`. Such a morphism has three parts:
- a `left` component, which is a morphism in the discrete category with a single object, so it carries no information;
- a `right` component, a morphism `h : f.right ⟶ g.right` in T;
- the commutativity condition, which reduces to `f.hom ≫ h = g.hom`.

This is exactly the standard notion of a morphism in the coslice category. The name `Under.Hom` is a type synonym that gives the category instance a distinguished hom type. It mirrors `Over.Hom` at line 44 and is already at full generality: any category T and any object X.
